The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and statements concerning South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2729 (2024) and 2731 (2024), and the statements of its President,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of South Sudan, and recalling the primary responsibility of the Government of South Sudan to protect its population and ensure the safety and security of all persons in South Sudan,

Recalling the Revitalized Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in the Republic of South Sudan done at Addis Ababa on 12 September 2018, and the need for its full and timely implementation,

Taking note of the decision to extend the transitional period to December 2026 and stressing the need for timely, transparent, and credible preparations for elections,

Expressing grave concern at the recent clashes in and around Nasir, Upper Nile State, and at continued insecurity in other parts of South Sudan, including attacks on civilians, killings, conflict-related sexual violence, displacement, and destruction of property,

Expressing concern at the humanitarian crisis in South Sudan, including food insecurity and the threat of famine, and stressing the need for safe, rapid, and unhindered humanitarian access to all those in need,

Expressing concern at the delays in implementation of the transitional security arrangements, constitution-making, and preparations for elections, and stressing the need for an inclusive political process and for free, fair, and credible elections in accordance with the Revitalized Agreement,

Reaffirming the importance of accountability for violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses, and of ending impunity,

Recalling the arms embargo and other measures imposed by resolution 2428 (2018) and renewed by subsequent resolutions, including resolution 2731 (2024), and calling for their full implementation,

Commending the work of the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS) and the outstanding contribution of troop- and police-contributing countries, and expressing deep concern at attacks on UNMISS personnel and humanitarian actors,

Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS) until 30 April 2026;

2. Decides that UNMISS shall include military, police, and civilian components and shall be mandated to:

(a) Protect civilians under imminent threat of physical violence, including by proactive and robust deployment, in particular in conflict-affected areas such as Upper Nile, Jonglei, and other areas of high risk, and to contribute to creating conditions conducive to the safe and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance;

(b) Monitor, investigate, verify, and report on violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses, including conflict-related sexual violence, and support efforts to strengthen accountability and the rule of law;

(c) Facilitate the safe, rapid, and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance by United Nations agencies, humanitarian partners, and other relevant actors, and protect humanitarian personnel and assets;

(d) Support the implementation of the Revitalized Agreement, including the permanent ceasefire and transitional security arrangements, and support inclusive political dialogue and national reconciliation;

(e) Support preparations for free, fair, and credible elections, in accordance with the Revitalized Agreement and upon request of the parties, including through good offices, technical assistance, and coordination with relevant stakeholders, while ensuring that such support is consistent with the mandate of UNMISS;

(f) Support the protection of civilians and promote respect for human rights, including by engaging with the Government of South Sudan, the opposition, and armed groups;

3. Authorizes UNMISS to take all necessary measures, within its capabilities and areas of deployment, to protect civilians and to carry out its mandate, and requests UNMISS to prioritize the protection of civilians;

4. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities, in particular in Nasir and Upper Nile State, exercise maximum restraint, and engage in dialogue through existing ceasefire mechanisms and political processes, and calls on all armed groups to refrain from violence and to resolve grievances peacefully;

5. Demands that all parties, including the Government of South Sudan, ensure safe, unhindered, and sustained access for UNMISS and humanitarian actors to all areas of South Sudan, and cooperate fully with UNMISS, including by ensuring the safety and security of its personnel and assets;

6. Condemns all attacks on civilians, humanitarian personnel, and United Nations personnel, and demands that the perpetrators of the recent clashes in Nasir and other violations be held accountable, including through independent and credible investigations and prosecution of those responsible;

7. Calls on the Government of South Sudan and the opposition to fully implement the Revitalized Agreement, including by expediting transitional security arrangements, completing the constitution-making process, creating the necessary conditions for inclusive and credible elections, and ensuring an enabling environment for civil society, media, and political actors, including freedom of expression and assembly;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to review the configuration and resources of UNMISS to ensure the efficient and effective implementation of its mandate, and to provide the Council with comprehensive reports every 90 days on the implementation of the UNMISS mandate, including on the protection of civilians, humanitarian access, human rights, political developments, and preparations for elections, and to include in those reports updates on the situation in Upper Nile and efforts to address the recent clashes in Nasir;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

